<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Simsir</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1395 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Muslim principality of the Chechens on the North Caucasus plain, known almost only from the chronicles of Timur's campaign of 1395.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Mongol conquest, Chechen communities on the plains north of the mountains came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. In the 14th century a principality called Simsir formed there, apparently around the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunzha">Sunzha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argun-caucasus">Argun</a> rivers. Its origins are not recorded.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Persian chroniclers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-din-ali-yazdi">Sharaf al-Din Yazdi</a>, mention its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghayur-khan">Ghayur Khan</a> and describe Simsir as a Muslim land, one of the first among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>. It paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> in his war against Timur.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1395 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur's</a> army devastated Simsir during its campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, and the principality disappears from the record. Its people withdrew to the mountains, and the plain was resettled only later.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/simsir.jpg" alt="Simsir" width="320" height="205"><figcaption>Image: Deni Mataev · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caucasus_about_year_1124.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1395 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ghayur Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chechen</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Simsim, Principality of Simsir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1348&amp;focus=simsir" aria-label="Simsir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5620 238 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Simsir lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6352 637h0M6582 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5940 494h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5940 494h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1348&amp;focus=simsir">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

